\begin{abstract}
The pion scalar radius,
\[
\langle r^{2}\rangle_{S}^{\pi}
 = 6\,\frac{d}{dt}\ln F_{S}^{\pi}(t)\bigg|_{t=0},
\]
is an important low-energy quantity entering precision analyses of chiral dynamics and \(\pi\pi\) scattering. We examine its dispersive determination from the scalar pion form factor \(F_{S}^{\pi}(t)\), with particular attention to the isoscalar \(S\)-wave. For classes of \(I=0\), \(S\)-wave \(T\)-matrices whose phase evolution crosses the relevant branch near the \(K\bar K\) region, a consistent Omnès representation requires a zero of the scalar form factor. Omitting this zero can produce an artificial enhancement of the inferred scalar radius and destabilize the connection between phase-shift information and low-energy observables. We emphasize that the resulting determination remains sensitive to uncertainties in the experimental \(\pi\pi\) phase shifts, especially in the inelastic region, as well as to assumptions concerning the high-energy phase and the asymptotic QCD behavior of the form factor. These effects must therefore be incorporated systematically before the scalar radius can be used as precision input for \(\pi\pi\)-scattering phenomenology and tests of chiral symmetry.
\end{abstract}
